helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	tit				0	sh	Using Variables				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149346				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>names of variables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>variables; using\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>types of variables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>declaring variables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>values;of variables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>constants\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>arrays;declaring\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>defining;constants\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149346				0	sh	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01020100.xhp\" name=\"Using Variables\"\>Using Variables\</link\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154346				0	sh	The following describes the basic use of variables in $[officename] Basic.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153361				0	sh	Naming Conventions for Variable Identifiers				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148797				0	sh	A variable name can consist of a maximum of 255 characters. The first character of a variable name \<emph\>must\</emph\> be a letter A-Z or a-z. Numbers can also be used in a variable name, but punctuation symbols and special characters are not permitted, with exception of the underscore character ("_"). In $[officename] Basic variable identifiers are not case-sensitive. Variable names may contain spaces but must be enclosed in square brackets if they do.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156422				0	sh	Examples for variable identifiers:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3163798				0	sh	MyNumber=5				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156441				0	sh	Correct				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147317				0	sh	MyNumber5=15				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149664				0	sh	Correct				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145364				0	sh	MyNumber_5=20				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146119				0	sh	Correct				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154729				0	sh	My Number=20				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153876				0	sh	Not valid, variable with space must be enclosed in square brackets				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147126				0	sh	[My Number]=12				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154510				0	sh	Correct				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153708				0	sh	DéjàVu=25				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150330				0	sh	Not valid, special characters are not allowed				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155443				0	sh	5MyNumber=12				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154254				0	sh	Not valid, variable may not begin with a number				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147345				0	sh	Number,Mine=12				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149256				0	sh	Not valid, punctuation marks are not allowed				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146317				0	sh	Declaring Variables				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150299	18			0	sh	In $[officename] Basic you don't need to declare variables explicitly. A variable declaration can be performed with the \<emph\>Dim\</emph\> statement. You can declare more than one variable at a time by separating the names with a comma. To define the variable type, use either a type-declaration sign after the name, or the appropriate key word. 				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154118				0	sh	Examples for variable declarations:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150090				0	sh	DIM a$				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150982				0	sh	Declares the variable "a" as a String				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149531				0	sh	DIM a As String				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150343				0	sh	Declares the variable "a" as a String				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149036				0	sh	DIM a$, b As Integer				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155507				0	sh	Declares one variable as a String and one as an Integer				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_idN10854				0	sh	DIM c As Boolean				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_idN10859				0	sh	Declares c as a Boolean variable that can be TRUE or FALSE				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150519				0	sh	It is very important when declaring variables that you use the type-declaration character each time, even if it was used in the declaration instead of a keyword. Thus the following statements are invalid:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152985				0	sh	DIM a$				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154527				0	sh	Declares "a" as a String				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148599				0	sh	a="TestString"				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153064				0	sh	Type-declaration missing: "a$="				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3144770				0	sh	Once you have declared a variable as a certain type, you cannot declare the variable under the same name again as a different type!				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149331				0	sh	Forcing Variable Declarations				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149443				0	sh	To force declaration of variables, use the following command:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152869				0	sh	OPTION EXPLICIT				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155072				0	sh	The \<emph\>Option Explicit\</emph\> statement has to be the first line in the module, before the first SUB. Generally, only arrays need to be declared explicitly. All other variables are declared according to the type-declaration character, or - if omitted - as the default type \<emph\>Single\</emph\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154614				0	sh	Variable Types				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155383				0	sh	$[officename] Basic supports four variable classes:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153972	36			0	sh	\<emph\>Numeric\</emph\> variables can contain number values. Some variables are used to store large or small numbers, and others are used for floating-point or fractional numbers. 				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3159226				0	sh	\<emph\>String\</emph\> variables contain character strings.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145217				0	sh	\<emph\>Boolean\</emph\> variables contain either the TRUE or the FALSE value.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154762				0	sh	\<emph\>Object\</emph\> variables can store objects of various types, like tables and documents within a document.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153805				0	sh	Integer Variables				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146966				0	sh	Integer variables range from -32768 to 32767. If you assign a floating-point value to an integer variable, the decimal places are rounded to the next integer. Integer variables are rapidly calculated in procedures and are suitable for counter variables in loops. An integer variable only requires two bytes of memory. "%" is the type-declaration character.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153810				0	sh	Dim Variable%				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153556				0	sh	Dim Variable As Integer				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147546				0	sh	Long Integer Variables				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151193				0	sh	Long integer variables range from -2147483648 to 2147483647. If you assign a floating-point value to a long integer variable, the decimal places are rounded to the next integer. Long integer variables are rapidly calculated in procedures and are suitable for counter variables in loops for large values. A long integer variable requires four bytes of memory. "&" is the type-declaration character.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154708				0	sh	Dim Variable&				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156365				0	sh	Dim Variable as Long				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id7596972				0	sh	Decimal Variables				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id2649311				0	sh	Decimal variables can take positive or negative numbers or zero. Accuracy is up to 29 digits.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id7617114				0	sh	You can use plus (+) or minus (-) signs as prefixes for decimal numbers (with or without spaces).				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id1593676				0	sh	If a decimal number is assigned to an integer variable, %PRODUCTNAME Basic rounds the figure up or down.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147500				0	sh	Single Variables				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153070				0	sh	Single variables can take positive or negative values ranging from 3.402823 x 10E38 to 1.401298 x 10E-45. Single variables are floating-point variables, in which the decimal precision decreases as the non-decimal part of the number increases. Single variables are suitable for mathematical calculations of average precision. Calculations require more time than for Integer variables, but are faster than calculations with Double variables. A Single variable requires 4 bytes of memory. The type-declaration character is "!".				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149875				0	sh	Dim Variable!				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153302				0	sh	Dim Variable as Single				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155753				0	sh	Double Variables				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150953				0	sh	Double variables can take positive or negative values ranging from 1.79769313486232 x 10E308 to 4.94065645841247 x 10E-324. Double variables are floating-point variables, in which the decimal precision decreases as the non-decimal part of the number increases. Double variables are suitable for precise calculations. Calculations require more time than for Single variables. A Double variable requires 8 bytes of memory. The type-declaration character is "#".				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150431				0	sh	Dim Variable#				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154406				0	sh	Dim Variable As Double				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155747				0	sh	Currency Variables				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153337				0	sh	Currency variables are internally stored as 64-bit numbers (8 Bytes) and displayed as a fixed-decimal number with 15 non-decimal and 4 decimal places. The values range from -922337203685477.5808 to +922337203685477.5807. Currency variables are used to calculate currency values with a high precision. The type-declaration character is "@".				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147296				0	sh	Dim Variable@				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150391				0	sh	Dim Variable As Currency				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148742				0	sh	String Variables				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151393				0	sh	String variables can hold character strings with up to 65,535 characters. Each character is stored as the corresponding Unicode value. String variables are suitable for word processing within programs and for temporary storage of any non-printable character up to a maximum length of 64 Kbytes. The memory required for storing string variables depends on the number of characters in the variable. The type-declaration character is "$".				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3166467				0	sh	Dim Variable$				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153027				0	sh	Dim Variable As String				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150534				0	sh	Boolean Variables				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145632				0	sh	Boolean variables store only one of two values: TRUE or FALSE. A number 0 evaluates to FALSE, every other value evaluates to TRUE.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147615				0	sh	Dim Variable As Boolean				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149722				0	sh	Date Variables				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3159116				0	sh	Date variables can only contain dates and time values stored in an internal format. Values assigned to Date variables with \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030101.xhp\" name=\"Dateserial\"\>\<emph\>Dateserial\</emph\>\</link\>, \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030102.xhp\" name=\"Datevalue\"\>\<emph\>Datevalue\</emph\>\</link\>, \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030205.xhp\" name=\"Timeserial\"\>\<emph\>Timeserial\</emph\>\</link\> or \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030206.xhp\" name=\"Timevalue\"\>\<emph\>Timevalue\</emph\>\</link\> are automatically converted to the internal format. Date-variables are converted to normal numbers by using the \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030103.xhp\" name=\"Day\"\>\<emph\>Day\</emph\>\</link\>, \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030104.xhp\" name=\"Month\"\>\<emph\>Month\</emph\>\</link\>, \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030106.xhp\" name=\"Year\"\>\<emph\>Year\</emph\>\</link\> or the \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030201.xhp\" name=\"Hour\"\>\<emph\>Hour\</emph\>\</link\>, \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030202.xhp\" name=\"Minute\"\>\<emph\>Minute\</emph\>\</link\>, \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030204.xhp\" name=\"Second\"\>\<emph\>Second\</emph\>\</link\> function. The internal format enables a comparison of date/time values by calculating the difference between two numbers. These variables can only be declared with the key word \<emph\>Date\</emph\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150462				0	sh	Dim Variable As Date				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148732				0	sh	Initial Variable Values				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154549				0	sh	As soon as the variable has been declared, it is automatically set to the "Null" value. Note the following conventions:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3143222				0	sh	\<emph\>Numeric\</emph\> variables are automatically assigned the value "0" as soon as they are declared.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150693				0	sh	\<emph\>Date variables\</emph\> are assigned the value 0 internally; equivalent to converting the value to "0" with the \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030103.xhp\" name=\"Day\"\>\<emph\>Day\</emph\>\</link\>, \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030104.xhp\" name=\"Month\"\>\<emph\>Month\</emph\>\</link\>, \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030106.xhp\" name=\"Year\"\>\<emph\>Year\</emph\>\</link\> or the \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030201.xhp\" name=\"Hour\"\>\<emph\>Hour\</emph\>\</link\>, \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030202.xhp\" name=\"Minute\"\>\<emph\>Minute\</emph\>\</link\>, \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030204.xhp\" name=\"Second\"\>\<emph\>Second\</emph\>\</link\> function.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154807				0	sh	\<emph\>String variables\</emph\> are assigned an empty-string ("") when they are declared.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153936				0	sh	Arrays				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148736				0	sh	$[officename] Basic knows one- or multi-dimensional arrays, defined by a specified variable type. Arrays are suitable for editing lists and tables in programs. Individual elements of an array can be addressed through a numeric index.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149546				0	sh	Arrays \<emph\>must\</emph\> be declared with the \<emph\>Dim\</emph\> statement. There are several ways to define the index range of an array:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150143				0	sh	DIM text$(20)				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154567				0	sh	21 elements numbered from 0 to 20				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145596				0	sh	DIM text$(5,4)				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154397				0	sh	30 elements (a matrix of 6 x 5 elements)				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149185				0	sh	DIM text$(5 to 25)				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149690				0	sh	21 elements numbered from 5 to 25				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155950				0	sh	DIM text$(-15 to 5)				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153113				0	sh	21 elements (including 0), numbered from -15 to 5				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153005				0	sh	The index range can include positive as well as negative numbers. 				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154507				0	sh	Constants				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156357				0	sh	Constants have a fixed value. They are only defined once in the program and cannot be redefined later:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153203				0	sh	CONST ConstName=Expression				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	tit				0	sh	The Basic Editor				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	bm_id3148647				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>saving;Basic code\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>loading;Basic code\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>Basic editor\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>navigating;in Basic projects\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>long lines;in Basic editor\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>lines of text;in Basic editor\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>continuation;long lines in editor\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147264				0	sh	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01030200.xhp\" name=\"The Basic Editor\"\>The Basic Editor\</link\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145069				0	sh	The Basic Editor provides the standard editing functions you are familiar with when working in a text document. It supports the functions of the \<emph\>Edit\</emph\> menu (Cut, Delete, Paste), the ability to select text with the Shift key, as well as cursor positioning functions (for example, moving from word to word with \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> and the arrow keys).				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154686				0	sh	Long lines can be split into several parts by inserting a space and an underline character _ as the last two characters of a line. This connects the line with the following line to one logical line. (If "Option Compatible" is used in the same Basic module, the line continuation feature is also valid for comment lines.)				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151042				0	sh	If you press the \<emph\>Run BASIC\</emph\> icon on the \<emph\>Macro\</emph\> bar, program execution starts at the first line of the Basic editor. The program executes the first Sub or Function and then program execution stops. The "Sub Main" does not take precedence on program execution.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	par_id59816				0	sh	Insert your Basic code between the Sub Main and End Sub lines that you see when you first open the IDE. Alternatively, delete all lines and then enter your own Basic code.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3125863				0	sh	Navigating in a Project				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145785				0	sh	The Library List				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	par_id3146120				0	sh	Select a library from the \<emph\>Library\</emph\> list at the left of the toolbar to load the library in the editor. The first module of the selected library will be displayed.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153190				0	sh	The Object Catalog				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148647				0	sh	Saving and Loading Basic Source Code				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154320				0	sh	You can save Basic code in a text file for saving and importing in other programming systems.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149959				0	sh	You cannot save Basic dialogs to a text file.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149403				0	sh	Saving Source Code to a Text File				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150327				0	sh	Select the module that you want to export as text from the object catalog.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150752				0	sh	Click the \<emph\>Save Source As\</emph\> icon in the Macro toolbar.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154754				0	sh	Select a file name and click \<emph\>OK\</emph\> to save the file.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159264				0	sh	Loading Source Code From a Text File				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147343				0	sh	Select the module where you want to import the source code from the object catalog.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145230				0	sh	Position the cursor where you want to insert the program code.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149565				0	sh	Click the \<emph\>Insert Source Text\</emph\> icon in the Macro toolbar.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154020				0	sh	Select the text file containing the source code and click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153198				0	sh	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01050000.xhp\" name=\"Basic IDE\"\>Basic IDE\</link\>				20181231 09:50:43
