helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03132300.xhp	0	help	par_id3147291	2			0	sh	Returns an object that represents a strictly typed value referring to the Uno type system. 				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03132300.xhp	0	help	par_id3143267				0	sh	This object is automatically converted to an Any of the corresponding type when passed to Uno. The type must be specified by its fully qualified Uno type name.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03132300.xhp	0	help	par_id3153626				0	sh	The $[officename] API frequently uses the Any type. It is the counterpart of the Variant type known from other environments. The Any type holds one arbitrary Uno type and is used in generic Uno interfaces.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03132300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147560				0	sh	Syntax:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03132300.xhp	0	help	par_id3154760				0	sh	oUnoValue = CreateUnoValue( "[]byte", MyBasicValue ) to get a byte sequence.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03132300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150541				0	sh	If CreateUnoValue cannot be converted to the specified Uno type, and error occurs. For the conversion, the TypeConverter service is used.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03132300.xhp	0	help	par_id3153524				0	sh	This function is intended for use in situations where the default Basic to Uno type converting mechanism is insufficient. This can happen when you try to access generic Any based interfaces, such as XPropertySet::setPropertyValue( Name, Value ) or X???Container::insertBy???( ???, Value ), from $[officename] Basic. The Basic runtime does not recognize these types as they are only defined in the corresponding service.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03132300.xhp	0	help	par_id3154366				0	sh	In this type of situation, $[officename] Basic chooses the best matching type for the Basic type that you want to convert. However, if the wrong type is selected, an error occurs. You use the CreateUnoValue() function to create a value for the unknown Uno type.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03132300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150769				0	sh	You can also use this function to pass non-Any values, but this is not recommend. If Basic already knows the target type, using the CreateUnoValue() function will only lead to additional converting operations that slow down the Basic execution.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\keys.xhp	0	help	tit				0	sh	Keyboard Shortcuts in the Basic IDE				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\keys.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154760				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>keyboard;in IDE\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>shortcut keys;Basic IDE\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>IDE;keyboard shortcuts\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\keys.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154760				0	sh	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/keys.xhp\" name=\"Keyboard Shortcuts in the Basic IDE\"\>Keyboard Shortcuts in the Basic IDE\</link\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\keys.xhp	0	help	par_id3149655				0	sh	In the Basic IDE you can use the following keyboard shortcuts:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\keys.xhp	0	help	par_id3154908				0	sh	Action				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\keys.xhp	0	help	par_id3153192				0	sh	Keyboard shortcut				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\keys.xhp	0	help	par_id3159254				0	sh	Run code starting from the first line, or from the current breakpoint, if the program stopped there before				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\keys.xhp	0	help	par_id3163712				0	sh	F5				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\keys.xhp	0	help	par_id3150010				0	sh	Stop				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\keys.xhp	0	help	par_id3154319				0	sh	Shift+F5				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\keys.xhp	0	help	par_id3151073				0	sh	Add \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01050100.xhp\" name=\"watch\"\>watch\</link\> for the variable at the cursor				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\keys.xhp	0	help	par_id3154731				0	sh	F7				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\keys.xhp	0	help	par_id3148455				0	sh	Single step through each statement, starting at the first line or at that statement where the program execution stopped before.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\keys.xhp	0	help	par_id3150716				0	sh	F8				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\keys.xhp	0	help	par_id3156275				0	sh	Single step as with F8, but a function call is considered to be only \<emph\>one\</emph\> statement				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\keys.xhp	0	help	par_id3153764				0	sh	Shift+F8				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\keys.xhp	0	help	par_id3150323				0	sh	Set or remove a \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01030300.xhp\" name=\"breakpoint\"\>breakpoint\</link\> at the current line or all breakpoints in the current selection				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\keys.xhp	0	help	par_id3147339				0	sh	F9				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\keys.xhp	0	help	par_id3153963				0	sh	Enable/disable the breakpoint at the current line or all breakpoints in the current selection				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\keys.xhp	0	help	par_id3155175				0	sh	Shift+F9				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\keys.xhp	0	help	par_id3154702				0	sh	A running macro can be aborted with Shift+\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+Q, also from outside of the Basic IDE. If you are inside the Basic IDE and the macro halts at a breakpoint, Shift+\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+Q stops execution of the macro, but you can recognize this only after the next F5, F8, or Shift+F8.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03060400.xhp	0	help	tit				0	sh	Not-Operator [Runtime]				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03060400.xhp	0	help	bm_id3156024				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>Not operator (logical)\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03060400.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156024				0	sh	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03060400.xhp\" name=\"Not-Operator [Runtime]\"\>Not-Operator [Runtime]\</link\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03060400.xhp	0	help	par_id3159414				0	sh	Negates an expression by inverting the bit values.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03060400.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149457				0	sh	Syntax:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03060400.xhp	0	help	par_id3150360				0	sh	Result = Not Expression				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03060400.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151211				0	sh	Parameters:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03060400.xhp	0	help	par_id3147228				0	sh	\<emph\>Result:\</emph\> Any numeric variable that contains the result of the negation.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03060400.xhp	0	help	par_id3154124				0	sh	\<emph\>Expression:\</emph\> Any expression that you want to negate.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03060400.xhp	0	help	par_id3150868				0	sh	When a Boolean expression is negated, the value True changes to False, and the value False changes to True.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03060400.xhp	0	help	par_id3145785				0	sh	In a bitwise negation each individual bit is inverted.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03060400.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153093				0	sh	Example:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03060400.xhp	0	help	par_id3153143				0	sh	Sub ExampleNot				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03060400.xhp	0	help	par_id3147317				0	sh	Dim vA as Variant, vB as Variant, vC as Variant, vD as Variant				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03060400.xhp	0	help	par_id3145274				0	sh	Dim vOut as Variant				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03060400.xhp	0	help	par_id3153363				0	sh	vA = 10: vB = 8: vC = 6: vD = Null				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03060400.xhp	0	help	par_id3145749				0	sh	vOut = Not vA REM Returns -11				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03060400.xhp	0	help	par_id3148645				0	sh	vOut = Not(vC > vD) REM Returns -1				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03060400.xhp	0	help	par_id3156441				0	sh	vOut = Not(vB > vA) REM Returns -1				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03060400.xhp	0	help	par_id3152596				0	sh	vOut = Not(vA > vB) REM Returns 0				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03060400.xhp	0	help	par_id3154319				0	sh	end Sub				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030100.xhp	0	help	tit				0	sh	Converting Date Values				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147573				0	sh	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030100.xhp\" name=\"Converting Date Values\"\>Converting Date Values\</link\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154760				0	sh	The following functions convert date values to calculable numbers and back.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030104.xhp	0	help	tit				0	sh	Month Function [Runtime]				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030104.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153127				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>Month function\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030104.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153127				0	sh	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030104.xhp\" name=\"Month Function [Runtime]\"\>Month Function [Runtime]\</link\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030104.xhp	0	help	par_id3148550				0	sh	Returns the month of a year from a serial date that is generated by the DateSerial or the DateValue function.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030104.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145068				0	sh	Syntax:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030104.xhp	0	help	par_id3150398				0	sh	Month (Number)				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030104.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154366				0	sh	Return value:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030104.xhp	0	help	par_id3154125				0	sh	Integer				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030104.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150768				0	sh	Parameters:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030104.xhp	0	help	par_id3156423				0	sh	\<emph\>Number:\</emph\> Numeric expression that contains the serial date number that is used to determine the month of the year.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030104.xhp	0	help	par_id3153770	9			0	sh	This function is the opposite of the \<emph\>DateSerial \</emph\>function. It returns the month in the year that corresponds to the serial date that is generated by \<emph\>DateSerial\</emph\> or \<emph\>DateValue\</emph\>. For example, the expression				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030104.xhp	0	help	par_id3147426				0	sh	Print Month(DateSerial(1994, 12, 20))				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030104.xhp	0	help	par_id3145366				0	sh	returns the value 12.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030104.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146923				0	sh	Example:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030104.xhp	0	help	par_id3156442				0	sh	Sub ExampleMonth				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030104.xhp	0	help	par_id3149664				0	sh	MsgBox "" & Month(Now) ,64,"The current month"				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030104.xhp	0	help	par_id3150012				0	sh	End sub				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	tit				0	sh	Format Function [Runtime]				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153539				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>Format function\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153539				0	sh	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03120301.xhp\" name=\"Format Function [Runtime]\"\>Format Function [Runtime]\</link\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3156042				0	sh	Converts a number to a string, and then formats it according to the format that you specify.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145090				0	sh	Syntax:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153527				0	sh	Format (Number [, Format As String])				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149178				0	sh	Return value:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3148474				0	sh	String				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159176				0	sh	Parameters:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3149415				0	sh	\<emph\>Number:\</emph\> Numeric expression that you want to convert to a formatted string.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3147531				0	sh	\<emph\>Format:\</emph\> String that specifies the format code for the number. If \<emph\>Format\</emph\> is omitted, the Format function works like the \<emph\>Str\</emph\> function.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147561				0	sh	Formatting Codes				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3147265				0	sh	The following list describes the codes that you can use for formatting a number:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153380				0	sh	\<emph\>0:\</emph\> If \<emph\>Number\</emph\> has a digit at the position of the 0 in the format code, the digit is displayed, otherwise a zero is displayed.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3151210				0	sh	If \<emph\>Number\</emph\> has fewer digits than the number of zeros in the format code, (on either side of the decimal), leading or trailing zeros are displayed. If the number has more digits to the left of the decimal separator than the amount of zeros in the format code, the additional digits are displayed without formatting.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3151176	14			0	sh	Decimal places in the number are rounded according to the number of zeros that appear after the decimal separator in the \<emph\>Format \</emph\>code.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3154123				0	sh	\<emph\>#:\</emph\> If \<emph\>Number\</emph\> contains a digit at the position of the # placeholder in the \<emph\>Format\</emph\> code, the digit is displayed, otherwise nothing is displayed at this position.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3148452	16			0	sh	This symbol works like the 0, except that leading or trailing zeroes are not displayed if there are more # characters in the format code than digits in the number. Only the relevant digits of the number are displayed.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3159150				0	sh	\<emph\>.:\</emph\> The decimal placeholder determines the number of decimal places to the left and right of the decimal separator.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3159252				0	sh	If the format code contains only # placeholders to the left of this symbol, numbers less than 1 begin with a decimal separator. To always display a leading zero with fractional numbers, use 0 as a placeholder for the first digit to the left of the decimal separator.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153368				0	sh	\<emph\>%:\</emph\> Multiplies the number by 100 and inserts the percent sign (%) where the number appears in the format code.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3149481				0	sh	\<emph\>E- E+ e- e+ :\</emph\> If the format code contains at least one digit placeholder (0 or #) to the right of the symbol E-, E+, e-, or e+, the number is formatted in the scientific or exponential format. The letter E or e is inserted between the number and the exponent. The number of placeholders for digits to the right of the symbol determines the number of digits in the exponent.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3149262				0	sh	If the exponent is negative, a minus sign is displayed directly before an exponent with E-, E+, e-, e+. If the exponent is positive, a plus sign is only displayed before exponents with E+ or e+.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3148617				0	sh	The thousands delimiter is displayed if the format code contains the delimiter enclosed by digit placeholders (0 or #).				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3163713				0	sh	The use of a period as a thousands and decimal separator is dependent on the regional setting. When you enter a number directly in Basic source code, always use a period as decimal delimiter. The actual character displayed as a decimal separator depends on the number format in your system settings.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3152887				0	sh	\<emph\>- + $ ( ) space:\</emph\> A plus (+), minus (-), dollar ($), space, or brackets entered directly in the format code is displayed as a literal character.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3148576				0	sh	To display characters other than the ones listed here, you must precede it by a backslash (\\), or enclose it in quotation marks (" ").				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153139				0	sh	\\ : The backslash displays the next character in the format code.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153366				0	sh	Characters in the format code that have a special meaning can only be displayed as literal characters if they are preceded by a backslash. The backslash itself is not displayed, unless you enter a double backslash (\\\\) in the format code.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3155411				0	sh	Characters that must be preceded by a backslash in the format code in order to be displayed as literal characters are date- and time-formatting characters (a, c, d, h, m, n, p, q, s, t, w, y, /, :), numeric-formatting characters (#, 0, %, E, e, comma, period), and string-formatting characters (@, &, <, >, !).				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3145749				0	sh	You can also use the following predefined number formats. Except for "General Number", all of the predefined format codes return the number as a decimal number with two decimal places.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3150113				0	sh	If you use predefined formats, the name of the format must be enclosed in quotation marks.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149377				0	sh	Predefined format				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3154730				0	sh	\<emph\>General Number:\</emph\> Numbers are displayed as entered.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153158				0	sh	\<emph\>Currency:\</emph\> Inserts a dollar sign in front of the number and encloses negative numbers in brackets.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3154490				0	sh	\<emph\>Fixed:\</emph\> Displays at least one digit in front of the decimal separator.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153415				0	sh	\<emph\>Standard:\</emph\> Displays numbers with a thousands separator.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3150715				0	sh	\<emph\>Percent:\</emph\> Multiplies the number by 100 and appends a percent sign to the number.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153836				0	sh	\<emph\>Scientific:\</emph\> Displays numbers in scientific format (for example, 1.00E+03 for 1000).				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153707				0	sh	A format code can be divided into three sections that are separated by semicolons. The first part defines the format for positive values, the second part for negative values, and the third part for zero. If you only specify one format code, it applies to all numbers.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149019				0	sh	Example:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3156054				0	sh	Sub ExampleFormat				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3148993				0	sh	MsgBox Format(6328.2, "##,##0.00")				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_idN107A2				0	sh	REM always use a period as decimal delimiter when you enter numbers in Basic source code.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3147339				0	sh	REM displays for example 6,328.20 in English locale, 6.328,20 in German locale.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3156382				0	sh	End Sub				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102400.xhp	0	help	tit				0	sh	IsEmpty Function [Runtime]				20181231 09:50:43
