helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	tit				0	sh	General Glossary				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154896				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>common terms;glossaries\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>glossaries;common terms\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>terminology;general glossary\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154896				0	sh	\<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000005.xhp\" name=\"General Glossary\"\>General Glossary\</link\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3154788				0	sh	This glossary includes explanations of some of the most important terms you will come across in $[officename].				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3154873				0	sh	Use the glossary to look up unfamiliar terms found in any $[officename] application.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	bm_id3156192				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>ASCII; definition\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156192				0	sh	ASCII				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3155922				0	sh	Abbreviation for American Standard Code for Information Interchange. ASCII is a character set for displaying fonts on personal computers. It consists of 128 characters including letters, numbers, punctuation and symbols. The extended ASCII character set contains 256 characters. Each character has been assigned a unique number, also referred to as ASCII Code.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3150823				0	sh	In HTML pages, only characters from the 7 Bit ASCII character set should appear. Other characters, such as German umlauts, are distinguished by way of a separate code. You can input extended ASCII code characters: the $[officename] export filter performs the necessary conversion.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151245				0	sh	Bézier Object				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3154924				0	sh	Developed by the French mathematician Pierre Bézier, a Bézier curve is a mathematically defined curve used in two-dimensional graphic applications. The curve is defined by four points: the initial position and the terminating position, and two separate middle points. Bézier objects can be modified by moving these points with the mouse.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	bm_id3146907				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>CTL;definition\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>complex text layout;definition\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>complex text layout, see CTL\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146907				0	sh	Complex Text Layout (CTL)				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3156081				0	sh	Languages with complex text layout may have some or all of the following features:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3145116				0	sh	The language is written with characters or glyphs that are composed of several parts				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3154630				0	sh	The text direction is from right to left.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3148677				0	sh	Currently, $[officename] supports Hindi, Thai, Hebrew, and Arabic as CTL languages.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3151176				0	sh	Enable CTL support using \<emph\>\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - Language Settings - Languages\</emph\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_idN108F1				0	sh	Context Menu				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	bm_id3147084				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>DDE; definition\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147084				0	sh	DDE				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3145154				0	sh	DDE stands for "Dynamic Data Exchange," which is a predecessor of OLE, "Object Linking and Embedding". With DDE, objects are linked through file reference, but not embedded.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3154820				0	sh	You can create a DDE link using the following procedure: Select cells from a Calc spreadsheet, copy them into the clipboard and switch to another spreadsheet and select the \<emph\>Edit - Paste Special\</emph\> dialog. Select \<emph\>the Link\</emph\> option to insert the contents as a DDE link. When activating a link, the inserted cell area will be read from its original file.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150439				0	sh	Direct and Style Formatting				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3159254				0	sh	If you format a document without Styles, it is referred to as "direct" formatting. This means modifying text or other objects, such as frames or tables, by applying various attributes directly. The format applies only to the selected area and all changes must be made separately. Styles, on the other hand, are not applied to the text directly, but rather are defined in the Styles and Formatting window and then applied. One advantage is that when you change a Style, all parts of the document to which that Style is assigned are modified at the same time.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3147287				0	sh	You can remove direct formatting from your document by selecting the entire text with the shortcut keys \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+A and then choosing \<emph\>Format - Default Formatting\</emph\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	bm_id3155132				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>windows; docking definition\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>docking; definition\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155132				0	sh	Docking				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3154638				0	sh	\<variable id=\"andock1\"\>Some windows in $[officename], for example the Styles and Formatting window and the Navigator, are "dockable" windows. You can move these windows, re-size them or dock them to an edge. On each edge you can dock several windows on top of, or alongside each other; then, by moving the border lines, you can change the relative proportions of the windows.\</variable\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3147233				0	sh	\<variable id=\"andock2\"\>To undock and re-dock, holding down the \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> key, double-click a vacant area in the window. In the Styles and Formatting window, you can also double-click a gray part of the window next to the icons, while you hold down the \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> key.\</variable\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155306				0	sh	Docking (AutoHide)				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3155854				0	sh	On any window edge where another window is docked you will see a button which allows you to show or hide the window.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3143274				0	sh	If you click the button on the window edge to show the window, the window will remain visible until you manually hide it again (with the same button).				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3153093				0	sh	If you show the window by clicking the window border, but not the button, you activate the \<emph\>AutoHide\</emph\> function. The AutoHide function allows you to temporarily show a hidden window by clicking on its edge. When you click in the document, the docked window hides again.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	bm_id3163710				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>formatting; definition\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3163710				0	sh	Formatting				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3163821				0	sh	Formatting refers to the visual layout of text using a word-processing or DTP program. This includes defining the paper format, page borders, fonts and font effects, as well as indents and spacing. You can format text \<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000005.xhp#Section7\"\>directly or with Styles\</link\> provided by $[officename].				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	bm_id3156006				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>IME; definition\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156006				0	sh	IME				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3157874				0	sh	IME stands for Input Method Editor. A program that allows the user to enter complex characters from non-western character sets using a standard keyboard.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	bm_id3151172				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>JDBC; definition\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151172				0	sh	JDBC				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3148386				0	sh	You can use the Java Database Connectivity (JDBC) API to connect to a database from %PRODUCTNAME. JDBC drivers are written in the Java programming language and are platform independent.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	bm_id3151282				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>kerning; definition\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151282				0	sh	Kerning				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3146321				0	sh	Kerning means increasing or decreasing the amount of space between pairs of letters to improve the overall appearance of the text.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3146078				0	sh	The kerning tables contain information on which pairs of letters require more spacing. These tables are generally a component of a font.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150592				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>links; definition\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150592				0	sh	Link				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3150092				0	sh	The \<emph\>Links\</emph\> command is found in the \<emph\>Edit\</emph\> menu. The command can only be activated when at least one link is contained in the current document. When you insert a picture, for example, you can either insert the picture directly into the document or insert the picture as a link.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3145730				0	sh	When an object is inserted directly into a document, the document size increases by (at least) the size in bytes of the object. You can save the document and open it on another computer, and the inserted object will still be in the same position in the document.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3144765				0	sh	If you insert the object as a link, only a reference to the file name is inserted. The file size of the document increases only by the path and file reference. If you open your document on another computer, however, the linked file must be in exactly the same position as given by the reference in order to view the object in the document.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3153334				0	sh	Use \<emph\>Edit - Links\</emph\> to see which files are inserted as links. The links can be removed if required. This will break the link and insert the object directly.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154512				0	sh	Number System				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3157846				0	sh	A number system is determined by the number of characters available for representing numbers. The decimal system, for instance is based on the ten numbers (0..9), the binary system is based on the two numbers 0 and 1, the hexadecimal system is based on 16 characters (0...9 and A...F).				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	bm_id3156358				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>objects; definition\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156358				0	sh	Object				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3144748				0	sh	An object is a screen element containing data. It can refer to application data, such as text or graphics.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3153839				0	sh	Objects are independent and do not influence each other. Any object containing data can be assigned certain commands. For example, a graphic object has commands for image editing and a spreadsheet contains calculation commands.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	bm_id3152827				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>ODBC; definition\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152827				0	sh	ODBC				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3153530				0	sh	Open Database Connectivity (ODBC) is a protocol norm with which applications can access database systems. The query language used is Structured Query Language (SQL). In $[officename], you can determine for each database whether to use SQL commands to run queries. Alternatively, you can use the interactive help to define your query by mouseclick and have it automatically translated into SQL by $[officename].				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3153956				0	sh	\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"WIN\"\>The 32bit ODBC functions required here can be installed on your system at any time with the help of the setup program supplied with your database. You can then amend the properties through the Control Panel. \</caseinline\>\</switchinline\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154479				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>OLE; definition\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154479				0	sh	OLE				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3157840				0	sh	Object Linking and Embedding (OLE) objects can be linked to a target document or may also be embedded. Embedding inserts a copy of the object and details of the source program in the target document. If you want to edit the object, simply activate the source program by double-clicking on the object.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154507				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>OpenGL; definition\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154507				0	sh	OpenGL				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3146879				0	sh	OpenGL represents a 3D graphics language, initially developed by SGI (Silicon Graphics Inc). Two dialects of this language are commonly used: Microsoft OpenGL, developed for use under Windows NT, and Cosmo OpenGL made by SGI. The latter represents an independent graphics language for all platforms and all kind of computers, even usable on machines without special 3-D graphics hardware.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155764				0	sh	PNG				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3148993				0	sh	Portable Network Graphics (PNG) is a graphic file format. The files are compressed with a selectable compression factor, and, as opposed to the JPG format, PNG files are always compressed without any information loss.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3083286				0	sh	Primary key				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3150323				0	sh	A primary key serves as a unique identifier of database fields. The unique identification of database fields is used in \<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000005.xhp#relational\"\>relational databases\</link\>, to access data in other tables. If reference is made to a primary key from another table, this is termed a foreign key.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3148916				0	sh	In $[officename], you define the primary key in the design view of a table, by choosing the relevant command from the context menu of a row header for the selected field.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147359				0	sh	Relational Database				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3147585	101			0	sh	A relational database is a collection of data items organized as a set of formally described tables from which data can be accessed or reassembled in many different ways without having to reorganize the database tables. 				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3154255				0	sh	A relational database management system (RDBMS) is a program that lets you create, update, and administer a relational database. An RDBMS takes Structured Query Language (SQL) statements entered by a user or contained in an application program and creates, updates, or provides access to the database.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3147535				0	sh	A good example of a relational database can be given with a database containing Customer, Purchase, and Invoice tables. In the Invoice table, there is no actual customer or purchasing data; however, the table contains references through a relational link, or a relation, to the respective customer and purchasing table's fields (for example, the customer ID field from the customer table).				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	bm_id3147315				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>register-true; definition\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147315				0	sh	Register-true				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3154223				0	sh	Register-true is a typography term that is used in printing. This term refers to the congruent imprint of the lines within a type area on the front and the back side of book pages, newspaper pages and magazine pages. The register-true feature make these pages easier to read by preventing gray shadows from shining through between the lines of text. The register-true term also refers to lines in adjacent text columns that are of the same height.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3145230				0	sh	When you define a paragraph, Paragraph Style, or a Page Style as register-true, the base lines of the affected characters are aligned to a vertical page grid, regardless of font size or of the presence of graphics. If you want, you can specify the setting for this grid as a Page Style property.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156710				0	sh	RTF				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3151186				0	sh	Rich Text Format (RTF) is a file format developed for the exchange of text files. A special feature is that the formatting is converted into directly readable text information. Unfortunately, in comparison to other file formats, this creates relatively large files.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156372				0	sh	Saving Relatively and Absolutely				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3146919				0	sh	In various dialogs (for example, \<emph\>Edit - AutoText\</emph\>) you can select whether you want to save files relatively or absolutely.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3152946				0	sh	If you choose to save relatively, the references to embedded graphics or other objects in your document will be saved relative to the location in the file system. In this case, it does not matter where the referenced directory structure is recorded. The files will be found regardless of location, as long as the reference remains on the same drive or volume. This is important if you want to make the document available to other computers that may have a completely different directory structure, drive or volume names. It is also recommended to save relatively if you want to create a directory structure on an Internet server.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3148927				0	sh	If you prefer absolute saving, all references to other files will also be defined as absolute, based on the respective drive, volume or root directory. The advantage is that the document containing the references can be moved to other directories or folders, and the references remain valid.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149922				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>SQL;definition\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149922				0	sh	SQL				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3152863				0	sh	Structured Query Language (SQL) is a language used for database queries. In $[officename] you can formulate queries either in SQL or interactively with the mouse.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147552				0	sh	SQL Database / SQL Server				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3159239				0	sh	An SQL database is a database system which offers an \<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000005.xhp#sql\"\>SQL\</link\> interface. SQL databases are often used in client/server networks in which different clients access a central server (for example, an SQL server), hence they are also called SQL server databases, or SQL servers for short.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3159118				0	sh	In $[officename], you can integrate external SQL databases. These may be located on your local hard disk as well as on the network. Access is achieved through \<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000005.xhp#odbc\"\>ODBC\</link\>, JDBC, or a native driver integrated into $[officename].				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3166423				0	sh	Widows and Orphans				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3149448				0	sh	Widows and orphans are historical typography terms, which have been in use for many years. A widow refers to a short line at the end of a paragraph, which when printed, appears alone at the top of the next page. An orphan is, in contrast, the first line of a paragraph printed alone at the bottom of the previous page. In a $[officename] text document you can automatically prevent such occurrences in the desired Paragraph Style. When doing so, you can determine the minimum amount of lines to be kept together on a page.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000206.xhp	0	help	tit				0	sh	Dif Import/Export/ Lotus import/ dBASE import				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000206.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155354				0	sh	Dif Import/Export/ Lotus import/ dBASE import				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000206.xhp	0	help	par_id3150620				0	sh	Defines the options for import/export. These dialogs will be automatically shown if the corresponding file type is selected.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000206.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149000				0	sh	Character set				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000206.xhp	0	help	par_id3152790				0	sh	\<ahelp hid=\"SC:LISTBOX:RID_SCDLG_IMPORTOPT:LB_FONT\"\>Select the character set from the options used for import/export.\</ahelp\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000206.xhp	0	help	par_id3152942				0	sh	For further information regarding filters, refer to the topic: \<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000020.xhp\" name=\"Information about Import and Export Filters\"\>Information about Import and Export Filters\</link\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	tit				0	sh	Text Import				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150960				0	sh	Text Import				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id3149987				0	sh	\<ahelp hid=\"SC:MODALDIALOG:RID_SCDLG_ASCII\"\>Sets the import options for delimited data.\</ahelp\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147588				0	sh	Import				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154788				0	sh	Character Set				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id3149495				0	sh	\<ahelp hid=\"SC:LISTBOX:RID_SCDLG_ASCII:LB_CHARSET\"\>Specifies the character set to be used in the imported file.\</ahelp\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	hd_id315478899				0	sh	Language				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id314949588				0	sh	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Determines how the number strings are imported.\</ahelp\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id314949587				0	sh	If Language is set to Default (for CSV import) or Automatic (for HTML import), Calc will use the globally set language. If Language is set to a specific language, that language will be used when importing numbers.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id314949586				0	sh	When importing an HTML document, the Language selection can conflict with the global HTML option \<link href=\"text/shared/optionen/01030500.xhp\" name=\"Use\"\>Use 'English (USA)' locale for numbers\</link\>. The global HTML option is effective only when the Automatic language option is selected. If you select a specific language in the HTML Import Options dialog, the global HTML option is ignored.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154894				0	sh	From Row				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id3150247				0	sh	\<ahelp hid=\"SC:NUMERICFIELD:RID_SCDLG_ASCII:NF_AT_ROW\"\>Specifies the row where you want to start the import.\</ahelp\> The rows are visible in the preview window at the bottom of the dialog.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149999				0	sh	Separator Options				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id3149640				0	sh	Specifies whether your data uses separators or fixed widths as delimiters.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156553				0	sh	Fixed width				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id3150710				0	sh	\<ahelp hid=\"SC:RADIOBUTTON:RID_SCDLG_ASCII:RB_FIXED\"\>Separates fixed-width data (equal number of characters) into columns.\</ahelp\> Click on the ruler in the preview window to set the width.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156560				0	sh	Separated by				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id3145136				0	sh	\<ahelp hid=\"SC:RADIOBUTTON:RID_SCDLG_ASCII:RB_SEPARATED\"\>Select the separator used in your data.\</ahelp\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147250				0	sh	Tab				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id3147576				0	sh	\<ahelp hid=\"SC:CHECKBOX:RID_SCDLG_ASCII:CKB_TAB\"\>Separates data delimited by tabs into columns.\</ahelp\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154317				0	sh	Semicolon				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id3157863				0	sh	\<ahelp hid=\"SC:CHECKBOX:RID_SCDLG_ASCII:CKB_SEMICOLON\"\>Separates data delimited by semicolons into columns.\</ahelp\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145313				0	sh	Comma				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id3150693				0	sh	\<ahelp hid=\"SC:CHECKBOX:RID_SCDLG_ASCII:CKB_COMMA\"\>Separates data delimited by commas into columns.\</ahelp\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	hd_id3163802				0	sh	Space				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id3153663				0	sh	\<ahelp hid=\"SC:CHECKBOX:RID_SCDLG_ASCII:CKB_SPACE\"\>Separates data delimited by spaces into columns.\</ahelp\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147335				0	sh	Other				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id3156329				0	sh	\<ahelp hid=\"SC:EDIT:RID_SCDLG_ASCII:ED_OTHER\"\>Separates data into columns using the custom separator that you specify. Note: The custom separator must also be contained in your data.\</ahelp\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150978				0	sh	Merge delimiters				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id3153827				0	sh	\<ahelp hid=\"SC:CHECKBOX:RID_SCDLG_ASCII:CB_ASONCE\"\>Combines consecutive delimiters and removes blank data fields.\</ahelp\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155341				0	sh	Text delimiter				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id3156326				0	sh	\<ahelp hid=\"SC:COMBOBOX:RID_SCDLG_ASCII:CB_TEXTSEP\"\>Select a character to delimit text data. You can can also enter a character in the text box.\</ahelp\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	hd_id315538811				0	sh	Other options				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id315366511				0	sh	Sets some other import options.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	hd_id314847411				0	sh	Quoted fields as text				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id314995711				0	sh	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>When this option is enabled, fields or cells whose values are quoted in their entirety (the first and last characters of the value equal the text delimiter) are imported as text.\</ahelp\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	hd_id314847422				0	sh	Detect special numbers				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id314995722				0	sh	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>When this option is enabled, Calc will automatically detect all number formats, including special number formats such as dates, time, and scientific notation.\</ahelp\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id314995723				0	sh	The selected language influences how such special numbers are detected, since different languages and regions many have different conventions for such special numbers.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id314995724				0	en-US	When this option is disabled, Calc will detect and convert only decimal numbers. The rest, including numbers formatted in scientific notation, will be imported as text. A decimal number string can have digits 0-9, thousands separators, and a decimal separator. Thousands separators and decimal separators may vary with the selected language and region.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id3149957				0	en-US	\<ahelp hid=\"SC:LISTBOX:RID_SCDLG_ASCII:LB_TYPE1\"\>Choose a column in the preview window and select the data type to be applied the imported data.\</ahelp\> You can select one of the following options:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155388				0	sh	Fields				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id3153665				0	sh	Shows how your data will look when it is separated into columns.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148474				0	sh	Column type				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id3152945				0	sh	Type				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id3145121				0	sh	Function				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id3149763				0	sh	Standard				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id3155923				0	sh	$[officename] determines the type.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id3152472				0	sh	Text				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id3148946				0	sh	Imported data are treated as text.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id3147265				0	sh	Date (DMY)				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id3156434				0	sh	Applies a date format (Day, Month, Year) to the imported data in a column.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id3148740				0	sh	Date (MDY)				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id3149688				0	sh	Applies a date format (Month, Day, Year) to the imported data in a column.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id3150230				0	sh	Date (YMD)				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id3153207				0	sh	Applies a date format (Year, Month, Day) to the imported data in a column.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id3148981				0	sh	US English				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id3153178				0	sh	Numbers formatted in US English are searched for and included regardless of the system language. A number format is not applied. If there are no US English entries, the \<emph\>Standard\</emph\> format is applied.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id3154329				0	sh	Hide				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id3154946				0	sh	The data in the column are not imported.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id3149767				0	sh	If you selected one of the date formats (DMY), (MDY), or (YMD) and you enter numbers without date delimiters, the numbers are interpreted as follows:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id3150650	76			0	sh	Number of characters 				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id3150495				0	sh	Date format				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id3156212				0	sh	6				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id3153056				0	sh	Two characters each are taken for day, month, and year in the selected order.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id3147352				0	sh	8				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id3153768				0	sh	Four characters are taken for the year, two each for month and day, in the selected order.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id3147295				0	sh	5 or 7				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id3151168				0	sh	As with 6 or 8 characters, but the first part of the sequence has one character less. This will suppress a leading zero for month and day.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id3153143				0	sh	If you want to include the leading zero in the data you import, in telephone numbers for example, apply the "Text" format to the column.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149287				0	sh	Preview				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id3147377				0	sh	Shows how the imported text will look after it is separated into columns. To apply a format to a column when it is imported, click a column and select a \<emph\>Column type\</emph\>. When you select a \<emph\>Column type\</emph\>, the column heading displays the applied format.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000208.xhp	0	help	par_id3150105				0	sh	If you want to use a fixed width to separate the imported data into columns, click in the ruler to set the width boundaries.				20181231 09:50:43
