helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150702				0	sh	\<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000002.xhp\" name=\"Glossary of Internet Terms\"\>Glossary of Internet Terms\</link\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3155577				0	sh	If you are a newcomer to the Internet, you will be confronted with unfamiliar terms: browser, bookmark, e-mail, homepage, search engine, and many others. To make your first steps easier, this glossary explains some of the more important terminology you may find in the Internet, intranet, mail and news.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153146				0	sh	Frames				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3157909				0	sh	Frames are useful for designing the layout of \<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000002.xhp#html\" name=\"HTML\"\>HTML\</link\> pages. $[officename] uses floating frames into which you can place objects such as graphics, movie files and sound. The context menu of a frame shows the options for restoring or editing frame contents. Some of these commands are also listed in \<emph\>Edit - Object\</emph\> when the frame is selected.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147077				0	sh	FTP				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3147335				0	sh	FTP stands for File Transfer Protocol and is the standard transfer protocol for files in the Internet. An FTP server is a program on a computer connected to the Internet which stores files to be transmitted with the aid of FTP. While FTP is responsible for transmitting and downloading Internet files, \<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000002.xhp#http\" name=\"HTTP\"\>HTTP\</link\> (Hypertext Transfer Protocol) provides the connection setup and data transfer between WWW servers and clients.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145609				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>HTML; definition\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145609				0	sh	HTML				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3161459				0	sh	HTML (Hypertext Markup Language) is a document code language, which is used as the file format for WWW documents. It is derived from \<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000002.xhp#sgml\" name=\"SGML\"\>SGML\</link\> and integrates text, graphics, videos and sound.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3154346	58			0	sh	If you want to type HTML commands directly, for example when doing exercises from one of the many available HTML books, remember that HTML pages are pure text files. Save your document under the document type \<emph\>Text \</emph\>and give it the file name extension .HTML. Be sure there are no umlauts or other special characters of the extended character set. If you want to re-open this file in $[officename] and edit the HTML code, you must load it with the file type \<emph\>Text\</emph\> and not with the file type \<emph\>Web pages\</emph\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3153960				0	sh	There are several references on the Internet providing an introduction to the HTML language.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147423				0	sh	HTTP				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3153379				0	sh	The Hypertext Transfer Protocol is a record of transmission of WWW documents between WWW servers (hosts) and browsers (clients).				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149290				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>hyperlinks; definition\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149290				0	sh	Hyperlink				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3145420				0	sh	Hyperlinks are cross-references, highlighted in text in various colors and activated by mouse-click. With the aid of hyperlinks, readers can jump to specific information within a document as well as to related information in other documents.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3156281				0	sh	In $[officename] you can assign hyperlinks to text as well as to graphics and text frames (see the Hyperlink Dialog icon on the Standard bar).				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	bm_id3152805				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>ImageMap; definition\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152805				0	sh	ImageMap				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3154685				0	sh	An ImageMap is a reference-sensitive graphic or text frame. You can click on defined areas of the graphic or text frame to go to a target (\<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000002.xhp#url\" name=\"URL\"\>URL\</link\>), which is linked with the area. The reference areas, along with the linked URLs and corresponding text displayed when resting the mouse pointer on these areas, are defined in the \<link href=\"text/shared/01/02220000.xhp\" name=\"ImageMap Editor\"\>ImageMap Editor\</link\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3153178				0	sh	There are two different types of ImageMaps. A Client Side ImageMap is evaluated on the client computer, which loaded the graphic from the Internet, while a Server Side ImageMap is evaluated on the server computer which provides the \<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000002.xhp#html\" name=\"HTML\"\>HTML\</link\> page on the Internet. In server evaluation, clicking an ImageMap sends the relative coordinates of the cursor within the image to the server, and a dedicated program on the server responds. In the client evaluation, clicking a defined hotspot of the ImageMap activates the URL, as if it were a normal text link. The URL appears below the mouse pointer when passing across the ImageMap.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3150740				0	sh	As ImageMaps can be used in different ways, they can be stored in different formats.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146874				0	sh	ImageMap Formats				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3145153				0	sh	ImageMaps are basically divided between those that are analyzed on the server (i. e. your Internet provider) and those analyzed on the web browser of the reader's computer.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	bm_id3152881				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>Server Side ImageMap\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152881				0	sh	Server Side ImageMaps				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3153057				0	sh	Server Side ImageMaps appear for the reader as a picture or frame on the page. Click on the ImageMap with the mouse, and the coordinates of the relative position are sent to the server. Aided by an extra program, the server then determines the next step to take. There are several incompatible methods to define this process, the two most common being:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3147502				0	sh	W3C (CERN) HTTP Server (Format type: MAP - CERN)				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3154011				0	sh	NCSA HTTP Server (Format type: MAP - NCSA)				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3149483	74			0	sh	$[officename] creates ImageMaps for both methods. Select the format from the \<emph\>File type \</emph\>list in the \<emph\>Save As \</emph\>dialog in the \<emph\>ImageMap Editor\</emph\>. Separate Map Files are created which you must upload to the server. You will need to ask your provider or network administrator which type of ImageMaps are supported by the server and how to access the evaluation program.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	bm_id3152418				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>Client Side ImageMap\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152418				0	sh	Client Side ImageMap				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3151290				0	sh	The area of the picture or frame where the reader can click is indicated by the appearance of the linked \<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000002.xhp#url\" name=\"URL\"\>URL\</link\> when the mouse passes over the area. The ImageMap is stored in a layer below the picture and contains information about the referenced regions. The only disadvantage of Client Side ImageMaps is that older Web browsers cannot read them; a disadvantage that will, however, resolve itself in time.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3149664				0	sh	When saving the ImageMap, select the file type \<emph\>SIP - StarView ImageMap\</emph\>. This saves the ImageMap directly in a format which can be applied to every active picture or frame in your document. However, if you just want to use the ImageMap on the current picture or text frame, you do not have to save it in any special format. After defining the regions, simply click \<emph\>Apply\</emph\>. Nothing more is necessary. Client Side ImageMaps saved in \<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000002.xhp#html\" name=\"HTML\"\>HTML\</link\> format are inserted directly into the page in HTML code.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	bm_id3159125				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>Java; definition\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159125				0	sh	Java				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3153188				0	sh	The Java programming language is a platform independent programming language that is especially suited for use in the Internet. Web pages and applications programmed with Java class files can be used on all modern operating systems. Programs using Java programming language are usually developed in a Java development environment and then compiled to a "byte code".				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	bm_id3159153				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>plug-ins; definition\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159153				0	sh	Plug-In				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3154127				0	sh	Extensions providing additional functionality in Web browsers are referred to as Plug-Ins.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3147484				0	sh	A Plug-In is a term used in various contexts:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3168608				0	sh	Plug-Ins in $[officename]				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3149910				0	sh	You will notice in $[officename] that the \<emph\>Formatting\</emph\> Bar changes after certain operations. For example, if you insert a formula into your text document, you see icons for editing the formula, in fact the same icons you see in formula documents. In this sense, we refer to the formula as a plug-in within the text document.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148387				0	sh	Using Plug-Ins to extend your programs				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3156737				0	sh	Plug-ins, generally speaking, are software additions to particular applications which provide enhanced functionality. Often import and export filters for various file formats are stored as plug-ins in a plug-in directory.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3149958				0	sh	Netscape web browser extensions produced by Netscape Communication Corporation are also called plug-ins. These are external programs mainly taken from the multimedia field and which communicate with the browser through standardized interfaces. These plug-ins can be linked to $[officename] documents.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3149420				0	sh	Any Netscape plug-ins (32 bit) installed on your system are automatically recognized by $[officename].				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145647				0	sh	Proxy				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3148455				0	sh	A proxy is a computer in the network acting as a kind of clipboard for data transfer. Whenever you access the Internet from a company network and request a Web page that has already been read by a colleague, the proxy will be able to display the page much quicker, as long as it's still in the memory. All that has to be checked in this case is that the page stored in the proxy is the latest version. If this is the case, the page won't have to be downloaded from the much slower Internet but can be loaded directly from the proxy.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154729				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>SGML; definition\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154729				0	sh	SGML				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3147330				0	sh	SGML stands for "Standard Generalized Markup Language". SGML is based on the idea that documents have structural and other semantic elements that can be described without reference to how such elements should be displayed. The actual display of such a document may vary, depending on the output medium and style preferences. In structured texts, SGML not only defines structures (in the DTD = Document Type Definition) but also ensures they are consistently used.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3148747				0	sh	\<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000002.xhp#html\" name=\"HTML\"\>HTML\</link\> is a specialized application of SGML. This means that most Web browsers support only a limited range of SGML standards and that almost all SGML-enabled systems can produce attractive HTML pages.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153950				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>search engines; definition\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153950				0	sh	Search Engines				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3157965				0	sh	A search engine is a service in the Internet based on a software program used to explore a vast amount of information using key words.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150751				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>tags; definition\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150751				0	sh	Tags				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3156360				0	sh	\<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000002.xhp#html\" name=\"HTML\"\>HTML\</link\> pages contain certain structural and formatting instructions called tags. Tags are code words enclosed by brackets in the document description language HTML. Many tags contain text or hyperlink references between the opening and closing brackets. For example, titles are marked by the tags <h1> at the beginning and </h1> at the end of the title. Some tags only appear on their own such as <br> for a line break or <img ...> to link a graphic.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153766				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>URL; definition\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153766				0	sh	URL				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3152931				0	sh	The Uniform Resource Locator (URL) displays the address of a document or a server in the Internet. The general structure of a URL varies according to type and is generally in the form Service://Hostname:Port/Path/Page#Mark although not all elements are always required. An URL can be a FTP address, a WWW (HTTP) address, a file address or an e-mail address.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000207.xhp	0	help	tit				0	sh	Export text files				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000207.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153116				0	sh	Export text files				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000207.xhp	0	help	par_id3150379				0	sh	The \<emph\>Export text files\</emph\> dialog allows you to define the export options for text files. The dialog will be displayed if you save spreadsheet data as file type "Text CSV", and if the \<emph\>Edit filter settings\</emph\> check box is marked in the \<emph\>Save As\</emph\> dialog.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000207.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155577				0	sh	Field options				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000207.xhp	0	help	par_id3152427				0	sh	Defines the field separator, text separator and character set that is used for the text export.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000207.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152876				0	sh	Character set				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000207.xhp	0	help	par_id3154689				0	sh	\<ahelp hid=\"SC:LISTBOX:RID_SCDLG_IMPORTOPT:DDLB_FONT\"\>Specifies the character set for text export.\</ahelp\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000207.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145138				0	sh	Field delimiter				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000207.xhp	0	help	par_id3150838				0	sh	\<ahelp hid=\"SC:COMBOBOX:RID_SCDLG_IMPORTOPT:ED_FIELDSEP\"\>Choose or enter the field delimiter, which separates data fields.\</ahelp\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000207.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154682				0	sh	Text delimiter				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000207.xhp	0	help	par_id3154863				0	sh	\<ahelp hid=\"SC:COMBOBOX:RID_SCDLG_IMPORTOPT:ED_TEXTSEP\"\>Choose or enter the text delimiter, which encloses every data field.\</ahelp\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000207.xhp	0	help	hd_id783149793				0	sh	Quote all text cells				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000207.xhp	0	help	par_id3152778363				0	sh	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Exports all text cells with leading and trailing quote characters as set in the Text delimiter box. If not checked, only those text cells get quoted that contain the Field delimiter character.\</ahelp\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000207.xhp	0	help	hd_id7145298				0	sh	Save cell content as shown				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000207.xhp	0	help	par_id5719779				0	sh	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Enabled by default, data will be saved as displayed, including applied number formats. If this checkbox is not marked, raw data content will be saved, as in older versions of the software.\</ahelp\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000207.xhp	0	help	par_id3541062				0	sh	Depending on the number format, saving cell content as shown may write values that during an import cannot be interpreted as numerical values anymore.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000207.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149793				0	sh	Fixed column width				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000207.xhp	0	help	par_id3152363				0	sh	\<ahelp hid=\"SC_CHECKBOX_RID_SCDLG_IMPORTOPT_CB_FIXEDWIDTH\"\>Exports all data fields with a fixed width.\</ahelp\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000207.xhp	0	help	par_id3149283				0	sh	The width of a data field in the exported text file is set to the current width of the corresponding column.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000207.xhp	0	help	par_id3154116				0	sh	Values are exported in the format as currently seen in the cell.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000207.xhp	0	help	par_id3156414				0	sh	If a value is longer than the fixed column width, it will be exported as a ### string.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000207.xhp	0	help	par_id3150178				0	sh	If a text string is longer than the fixed column width, it will be truncated at the end.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000207.xhp	0	help	par_id3148548				0	sh	The alignment Left, Centered, and Right will be simulated by inserted blanks.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\01000000.xhp	0	help	tit				0	sh	Showing and Hiding Docked Windows				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\01000000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3085157				0	sh	Showing and Hiding Docked Windows				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\01000000.xhp	0	help	par_id3149948				0	sh	Every \<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000005.xhp#andocken\" name=\"docked\"\>docked\</link\> window has an icon to control the display properties of the window.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\01000000.xhp	0	help	par_id3150502				0	sh	To show or hide a docked window, click the icon.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\01000000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150465				0	sh	AutoShow and AutoHide Docked Windows				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\01000000.xhp	0	help	par_id3155504				0	sh	You can click the edge of a hidden docked window to open the window.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\01000000.xhp	0	help	par_id3153257				0	sh	The docked window closes automatically when you move the mouse pointer outside of the window.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\01000000.xhp	0	help	par_id3154046				0	sh	Multiple docked windows act as a single window in AutoShow/AutoHide mode.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\01000000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145416				0	sh	Drag and Drop				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\01000000.xhp	0	help	par_id3149578				0	sh	If you drag an object over the edge of a hidden docked window, the window opens in AutoShow mode.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000007.xhp	0	help	tit				0	sh	Toolbars				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000007.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155620				0	sh	Toolbars				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000007.xhp	0	help	par_id3152823	4			0	sh	\<variable id=\"werkzeugleiste\"\>Icon on the Tools bar: \</variable\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000007.xhp	0	help	par_id3152352	5			0	sh	\<variable id=\"textobjektleiste\"\>Icon on the Formatting Bar: \</variable\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000007.xhp	0	help	par_id3151370	7			0	sh	\<variable id=\"objektleiste\"\>Icon on the Formatting Bar: \</variable\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000007.xhp	0	help	par_id3149748	9			0	sh	\<variable id=\"diaobjektleiste\"\>Icon on the Slide View Bar: \</variable\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000007.xhp	0	help	par_id3156553				0	sh	\<variable id=\"symbolleistenneu\"\>This overview describes the default toolbar configuration for $[officename].\</variable\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000007.xhp	0	help	par_id3153551				0	sh	Asian Language Support				20181231 09:50:43
