helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot.xhp	0	help	tit				0	sh	Pivot Table				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150448				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>pivot table function; introduction\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>DataPilot, see pivot table function\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150448				0	sh	\<variable id=\"datapilot\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/datapilot.xhp\" name=\"Pivot Table\"\>Pivot Table\</link\>\</variable\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot.xhp	0	help	par_id3156024				0	sh	The \<emph\>pivot table\</emph\> (formerly known as \<emph\>DataPilot\</emph\>) allows you to combine, compare, and analyze large amounts of data. You can view different summaries of the source data, you can display the details of areas of interest, and you can create reports.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot.xhp	0	help	par_id3145069				0	sh	A table that has been created as a \<link href=\"text/scalc/01/12090000.xhp\" name=\"pivot table\"\>pivot table\</link\> is an interactive table. Data can be arranged, rearranged or summarized according to different points of view.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_edittable.xhp	0	help	tit				0	sh	Editing Pivot Tables				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_edittable.xhp	0	help	bm_id3148663				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>pivot table function; editing tables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>editing;pivot tables\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_edittable.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148663				0	sh	\<variable id=\"datapilot_edittable\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/datapilot_edittable.xhp\" name=\"Editing Pivot Tables\"\>Editing Pivot Tables\</link\>\</variable\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_edittable.xhp	0	help	par_id3150868				0	sh	Click one of the buttons in the pivot table and hold the mouse button down. A special symbol will appear next to the mouse pointer.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_edittable.xhp	0	help	par_id3145786				0	sh	By dragging the button to a different position in the same row you can alter the order of the columns. If you drag a button to the left edge of the table into the row headings area, you can change a column into a row.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_edittable.xhp	0	help	par_id1648915				0	sh	In the Pivot Table dialog, you can drag a button to the \<emph\>Page Fields\</emph\> area to create a button and a listbox on top of the pivot table. The listbox can be used to filter the pivot table by the contents of the selected item. You can use drag-and-drop within the pivot table to use another page field as a filter.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_edittable.xhp	0	help	par_id3147434				0	sh	To remove a button from the table, just drag it out of the pivot table. Release the mouse button when the mouse pointer positioned within the sheet has become a 'not allowed' icon. The button is deleted.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_edittable.xhp	0	help	par_id3156442				0	sh	To edit the pivot table, click a cell inside the pivot table and open the context menu. In the context menu you find the command \<emph\>Edit Layout\</emph\>, which displays the \<emph\>Pivot Table\</emph\> dialog for the current pivot table.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_edittable.xhp	0	help	par_id2666096				0	sh	In the pivot table, you can use drag-and-drop or cut/paste commands to rearrange the order of data fields.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_edittable.xhp	0	help	par_id266609688				0	sh	You can assign custom display names to fields, field members, subtotals (with some restrictions), and grand totals inside pivot tables. A custom display name is assigned to an item by overwriting the original name with another name.				20181231 09:50:43
