helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12090105.xhp	0	help	par_id3149403				0	sh	\<ahelp hid=\"SC:CHECKBOX:RID_SCDLG_PIVOTSUBT:CB_SHOWALL\"\>Includes empty columns and rows in the results table.\</ahelp\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12090105.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149122				0	sh	Name:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12090105.xhp	0	help	par_id3150749				0	sh	Lists the name of the selected data field.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12090105.xhp	0	help	par_idN106EC				0	sh	More				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12090105.xhp	0	help	par_idN106F0				0	sh	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Expands or reduces the dialog. The \<emph\>More\</emph\> button is visible for data fields only.\</ahelp\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12090105.xhp	0	help	par_idN106F3				0	sh	Options				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12090105.xhp	0	help	par_idN106F7				0	sh	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Opens the \<link href=\"text/scalc/01/12090106.xhp\"\>Data Field Options\</link\> dialog. The \<emph\>Options\</emph\> button is visible for column, row, or page fields only.\</ahelp\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12090105.xhp	0	help	par_idN10708				0	sh	If the dialog is expanded by the \<emph\>More\</emph\> button, the following items are added to the dialog:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12090105.xhp	0	help	par_idN1070B				0	sh	Displayed value				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12090105.xhp	0	help	par_idN1070F				0	sh	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>For each data field, you can select the type of display.\</ahelp\> For some types you can select additional information for a base field and a base item.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12090105.xhp	0	help	par_idN10712				0	sh	Type				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12090105.xhp	0	help	par_idN10716				0	sh	\<ahelp hid=\"1495371266\"\>Select the type of calculating of the displayed value for the data field.\</ahelp\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12090105.xhp	0	help	par_idN10724				0	sh	Type				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12090105.xhp	0	help	par_idN1072A				0	sh	Displayed value				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12090105.xhp	0	help	par_idN10731				0	sh	Normal				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12090105.xhp	0	help	par_idN10737				0	sh	Results are shown unchanged				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12090105.xhp	0	help	par_idN1073E				0	sh	Difference from				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12090105.xhp	0	help	par_idN10744				0	sh	From each result, its reference value (see below) is subtracted, and the difference is shown. Totals outside of the base field are shown as empty results.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12090105.xhp	0	help	par_idN10747				0	sh	\<emph\>Named item\</emph\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12090105.xhp	0	help	par_idN1074C				0	sh	If a base item name is specified, the reference value for a combination of field items is the result where the item in the base field is replaced by the specified base item.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12090105.xhp	0	help	par_idN1074F				0	sh	\<emph\>Previous item or Next item\</emph\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12090105.xhp	0	help	par_idN10754				0	sh	If "previous item" or "next item" is specified as the base item, the reference value is the result for the next visible member of the base field, in the base field's sort order.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12090105.xhp	0	help	par_idN1075B				0	sh	% Of				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12090105.xhp	0	help	par_idN10761				0	sh	Each result is divided by its reference value. The reference value is determined in the same way as for "Difference from". Totals outside of the base field are shown as empty results. 				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12090105.xhp	0	help	par_idN1076A				0	sh	% Difference from				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12090105.xhp	0	help	par_idN10770				0	sh	From each result, its reference value is subtracted, and the difference is divided by the reference value. The reference value is determined in the same way as for "Difference from". Totals outside of the base field are shown as empty results.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12090105.xhp	0	help	par_idN10777				0	sh	Running total in				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12090105.xhp	0	help	par_idN1077D				0	sh	Each result is added to the sum of the results for preceding items in the base field, in the base field's sort order, and the total sum is shown.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12090105.xhp	0	help	par_idN10780				0	sh	Results are always summed, even if a different summary function was used to get each result.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12090105.xhp	0	help	par_idN10787				0	sh	% of row				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12090105.xhp	0	help	par_idN1078D				0	sh	Each result is divided by the total result for its row in the pivot table. If there are several data fields, the total for the result's data field is used. If there are subtotals with manually selected summary functions, the total with the data field's summary function is still used. 				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12090105.xhp	0	help	par_idN10794				0	sh	% of column				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12090105.xhp	0	help	par_idN1079A				0	sh	Same as "% of row", but the total for the result's column is used.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12090105.xhp	0	help	par_idN107A1				0	sh	% of total				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12090105.xhp	0	help	par_idN107A7				0	sh	Same as "% of row", but the grand total for the result's data field is used.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12090105.xhp	0	help	par_idN107AE				0	sh	Index				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12090105.xhp	0	help	par_idN107B4				0	sh	The row and column totals and the grand total, following the same rules as above, are used to calculate the following expression:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12090105.xhp	0	help	par_idN107B7				0	sh	( original result * grand total ) / ( row total * column total )				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12090105.xhp	0	help	par_idN107BA				0	sh	Base field				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12090105.xhp	0	help	par_idN107BE				0	sh	\<ahelp hid=\"1495371267\"\>Select the field from which the respective value is taken as base for the calculation.\</ahelp\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12090105.xhp	0	help	par_idN107C1				0	sh	Base item				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12090105.xhp	0	help	par_idN107C5				0	sh	\<ahelp hid=\"1495371268\"\>Select the item of the base field from which the respective value is taken as base for the calculation.\</ahelp\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	tit				0	sh	Statistical Functions Part One				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146320				0	sh	\<variable id=\"ae\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04060181.xhp\"\>Statistical Functions Part One\</link\>\</variable\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145632				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>INTERCEPT function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>points of intersection\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>intersections\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145632				0	sh	INTERCEPT				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3146887				0	sh	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_ACHSENABSCHNITT\"\>Calculates the point at which a line will intersect the y-values by using known x-values and y-values.\</ahelp\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150374				0	sh	Syntax				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149718				0	sh	INTERCEPT(DataY; DataX)				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149947	6			0	sh	            \<emph\>DataY\</emph\> is the dependent set of observations or data.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3147412	7			0	sh	            \<emph\>DataX\</emph\> is the independent set of observations or data.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3152983				0	sh	Names, arrays or references containing numbers must be used here. Numbers can also be entered directly.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3157906				0	sh	Example				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3148728				0	sh	To calculate the intercept, use cells D3:D9 as the y value and C3:C9 as the x value from the example spreadsheet. Input will be as follows:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149013	11			0	sh	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=INTERCEPT(D3:D9;C3:C9)\</item\> = 2.15.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3148437				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>COUNT function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>numbers;counting\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148437				0	sh	COUNT				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150700				0	sh	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_ANZAHL\"\>Counts how many numbers are in the list of arguments.\</ahelp\> Text entries are ignored.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153930				0	sh	Syntax				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3148585				0	sh	COUNT(Value1; Value2; ... Value30)				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3155827	17			0	sh	            \<emph\>Value1; Value2, ...\</emph\> are 1 to 30 values or ranges representing the values to be counted.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149254				0	sh	Example				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149953				0	sh	The entries 2, 4, 6 and eight in the Value 1-4 fields are to be counted.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3154558	20			0	sh	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=COUNT(2;4;6;"eight")\</item\> = 3. The count of numbers is therefore 3.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149729				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>COUNTA function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>number of entries\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149729				0	sh	COUNTA				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150142				0	sh	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_ANZAHL2\"\>Counts how many values are in the list of arguments.\</ahelp\> Text entries are also counted, even when they contain an empty string of length 0. If an argument is an array or reference, empty cells within the array or reference are ignored.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148573				0	sh	Syntax				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3153111				0	sh	COUNTA(Value1; Value2; ... Value30)				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150001	26			0	sh	            \<emph\>Value1; Value2, ...\</emph\> are 1 to 30 arguments representing the values to be counted.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150334				0	sh	Example				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3154508				0	sh	The entries 2, 4, 6 and eight in the Value 1-4 fields are to be counted.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3158000	29			0	sh	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=COUNTA(2;4;6;"eight")\</item\> = 4. The count of values is therefore 4.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150267				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>B function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>probabilities of samples with binomial distribution\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150267				0	sh	B				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3156061				0	sh	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_B\"\>Returns the probability of a sample with binomial distribution.\</ahelp\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150659				0	sh	Syntax				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3148392				0	sh	B(Trials; SP; T1; T2)				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149002	35			0	sh	            \<emph\>Trials\</emph\> is the number of independent trials.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3148875	36			0	sh	            \<emph\>SP\</emph\> is the probability of success on each trial.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3145352	37			0	sh	            \<emph\>T1\</emph\> defines the lower limit for the number of trials.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149538	38			0	sh	            \<emph\>T2\</emph\> (optional) defines the upper limit for the number of trials.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148768				0	sh	Example				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3154633				0	sh	What is the probability with ten throws of the dice, that a six will come up exactly twice? The probability of a six (or any other number) is 1/6. The following formula combines these factors:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149393	41			0	sh	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=B(10;1/6;2)\</item\> returns a probability of 29%.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3158416				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>RSQ function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>determination coefficients\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>regression analysis\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3158416				0	sh	RSQ				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3154949				0	sh	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_BESTIMMTHEITSMASS\"\>Returns the square of the Pearson correlation coefficient based on the given values.\</ahelp\> RSQ (also called determination coefficient) is a measure for the accuracy of an adjustment and can be used to produce a regression analysis.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152820				0	sh	Syntax				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3155822				0	sh	RSQ(DataY; DataX)				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150470	47			0	sh	            \<emph\>DataY\</emph\> is an array or range of data points.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3153181	48			0	sh	            \<emph\>DataX\</emph\> is an array or range of data points.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156258				0	sh	Example				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3155991	50			0	sh	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=RSQ(A1:A20;B1:B20)\</item\> calculates the determination coefficient for both data sets in columns A and B.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145620				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>BETAINV function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>cumulative probability density function;inverse of\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145620				0	sh	BETAINV				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149825				0	sh	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_BETAINV\"\>Returns the inverse of the cumulative beta probability density function.\</ahelp\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152479				0	sh	Syntax				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3156300				0	sh	BETAINV(Number; Alpha; Beta; Start; End)				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149266	56			0	sh	            \<emph\>Number\</emph\> is the value between \<emph\>Start\</emph\> and \<emph\>End\</emph\> at which to evaluate the function.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149710	57			0	sh	            \<emph\>Alpha\</emph\> is a parameter to the distribution.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3156306	58			0	sh	            \<emph\>Beta\</emph\> is a parameter to the distribution.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150960	59			0	sh	            \<emph\>Start\</emph\> (optional) is the lower bound for \<emph\>Number\</emph\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3151268	60			0	sh	            \<emph\>End\</emph\> (optional) is the upper bound for \<emph\>Number\</emph\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_idN109DF				0	sh	            \<embedvar href=\"text/scalc/00/00000004.xhp#optional\"/\>         				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147077				0	sh	Example				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3146859	62			0	sh	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=BETAINV(0.5;5;10)\</item\> returns the value 0.33.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3156096				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>BETADIST function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>cumulative probability density function;calculating\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156096				0	sh	BETADIST				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150880				0	sh	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_BETAVERT\"\>Returns the beta function.\</ahelp\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150762				0	sh	Syntax				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3147571				0	sh	BETADIST(Number; Alpha; Beta; Start; End; Cumulative)				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3156317	68			0	sh	            \<emph\>Number\</emph\> is the value between \<emph\>Start\</emph\> and \<emph\>End\</emph\> at which to evaluate the function.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3156107	69			0	sh	            \<emph\>Alpha\</emph\> is a parameter to the distribution.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3153619	70			0	sh	            \<emph\>Beta\</emph\> is a parameter to the distribution.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150254	71			0	sh	            \<emph\>Start\</emph\> (optional) is the lower bound for \<emph\>Number\</emph\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149138	72			0	sh	            \<emph\>End\</emph\> (optional) is the upper bound for \<emph\>Number\</emph\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id012020091254453				0	sh	            \<emph\>Cumulative\</emph\> (optional) can be 0 or False to calculate the probability density function. It can be any other value or True or omitted to calculate the cumulative distribution function.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_idN10AB3				0	sh	            \<embedvar href=\"text/scalc/00/00000004.xhp#optional\"/\>         				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145649				0	sh	Example				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3156118	74			0	sh	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=BETADIST(0.75;3;4)\</item\> returns the value 0.96				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3143228				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>BINOMDIST function\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3143228				0	sh	BINOMDIST				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3146897				0	sh	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_BINOMVERT\"\>Returns the individual term binomial distribution probability.\</ahelp\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149289				0	sh	Syntax				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3156009				0	sh	BINOMDIST(X; Trials; SP; C)				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3154304	80			0	sh	            \<emph\>X\</emph\> is the number of successes in a set of trials.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3147492	81			0	sh	            \<emph\>Trials\</emph\> is the number of independent trials.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3146085	82			0	sh	            \<emph\>SP\</emph\> is the probability of success on each trial.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149760	83			0	sh	            \<emph\>C\</emph\> = 0 calculates the probability of a single event and \<emph\>C\</emph\> = 1 calculates the cumulative probability.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151171				0	sh	Example				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3145666	85			0	sh	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=BINOMDIST(A1;12;0.5;0)\</item\> shows (if the values \<item type=\"input\"\>0\</item\> to \<item type=\"input\"\>12\</item\> are entered in A1) the probabilities for 12 flips of a coin that \<emph\>Heads\</emph\> will come up exactly the number of times entered in A1.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150120	86			0	sh	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=BINOMDIST(A1;12;0.5;1)\</item\> shows the cumulative probabilities for the same series. For example, if A1 = \<item type=\"input\"\>4\</item\>, the cumulative probability of the series is 0, 1, 2, 3 or 4 times \<emph\>Heads\</emph\> (non-exclusive OR).				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id0119200902432928				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>CHISQINV function\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id0119200902421451				0	sh	CHISQINV				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id0119200902421449				0	sh	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Returns the inverse of CHISQDIST.\</ahelp\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id0119200902475241				0	sh	Syntax				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id0119200902475286				0	sh	            \<emph\>Probability\</emph\> is the probability value for which the inverse of the chi-square distribution is to be calculated.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id0119200902475282				0	sh	            \<emph\>Degrees Of Freedom\</emph\> is the degrees of freedom for the chi-square function.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3148835				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>CHIINV function\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148835				0	sh	CHIINV				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149906				0	sh	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_CHIINV\"\>Returns the inverse of the one-tailed probability of the chi-squared distribution.\</ahelp\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159157				0	sh	Syntax				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150504				0	sh	CHIINV(Number; DegreesFreedom)				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3154898	92			0	sh	            \<emph\>Number\</emph\> is the value of the error probability.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3154294	93			0	sh	            \<emph\>DegreesFreedom\</emph\> is the degrees of freedom of the experiment.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154208				0	sh	Example				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150777				0	sh	A die is thrown 1020 times. The numbers on the die 1 through 6 come up 195, 151, 148, 189, 183 and 154 times (observation values). The hypothesis that the die is not fixed is to be tested.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3153062				0	sh	The Chi square distribution of the random sample is determined by the formula given above. Since the expected value for a given number on the die for n throws is n times 1/6, thus 1020/6 = 170, the formula returns a Chi square value of 13.27.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3148806				0	sh	If the (observed) Chi square is greater than or equal to the (theoretical) Chi square CHIINV, the hypothesis will be discarded, since the deviation between theory and experiment is too great. If the observed Chi square is less that CHIINV, the hypothesis is confirmed with the indicated probability of error.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149763	95			0	sh	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=CHIINV(0.05;5)\</item\> returns 11.07.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3159142	133			0	sh	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=CHIINV(0.02;5)\</item\> returns 13.39.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3158401				0	sh	If the probability of error is 5%, the die is not true. If the probability of error is 2%, there is no reason to believe it is fixed.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154260				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>CHITEST function\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154260				0	sh	CHITEST				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3151052				0	sh	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_CHITEST\"\>Returns the probability of a deviance from a random distribution of two test series based on the chi-squared test for independence.\</ahelp\> CHITEST returns the chi-squared distribution of the data.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3148925				0	sh	The probability determined by CHITEST can also be determined with CHIDIST, in which case the Chi square of the random sample must then be passed as a parameter instead of the data row.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154280				0	sh	Syntax				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149162				0	sh	CHITEST(DataB; DataE)				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3158421	101			0	sh	            \<emph\>DataB\</emph\> is the array of the observations.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3166453	102			0	sh	            \<emph\>DataE\</emph\> is the range of the expected values.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146946				0	sh	Example				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3154096				0	sh	Data_B (observed)				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3152948				0	sh	Data_E (expected)				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3152876				0	sh	1				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3159279	139			0	sh	                     \<item type=\"input\"\>195\</item\>                  				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149105	140			0	sh	                     \<item type=\"input\"\>170\</item\>                  				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149922				0	sh	2				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3148621	142			0	sh	                     \<item type=\"input\"\>151\</item\>                  				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3148987	143			0	sh	                     \<item type=\"input\"\>170\</item\>                  				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149417				0	sh	3				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3148661	145			0	sh	                     \<item type=\"input\"\>148\</item\>                  				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3151128	146			0	sh	                     \<item type=\"input\"\>170\</item\>                  				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3148467				0	sh	4				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149237	148			0	sh	                     \<item type=\"input\"\>189\</item\>                  				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3145304	149			0	sh	                     \<item type=\"input\"\>170\</item\>                  				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149927				0	sh	5				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150630	151			0	sh	                     \<item type=\"input\"\>183\</item\>                  				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150423	152			0	sh	                     \<item type=\"input\"\>170\</item\>                  				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3143275				0	sh	6				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3144750	154			0	sh	                     \<item type=\"input\"\>154\</item\>                  				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3153947	155			0	sh	                     \<item type=\"input\"\>170\</item\>                  				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149481	104			0	sh	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=CHITEST(A1:A6;B1:B6)\</item\> equals 0.02. This is the probability which suffices the observed data of the theoretical Chi-square distribution.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3148690				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>CHIDIST function\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148690				0	sh	CHIDIST				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3156338				0	sh	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_CHIVERT\"\>Returns the probability value from the indicated Chi square that a hypothesis is confirmed.\</ahelp\> CHIDIST compares the Chi square value to be given for a random sample that is calculated from the sum of (observed value-expected value)^2/expected value for all values with the theoretical Chi square distribution and determines from this the probability of error for the hypothesis to be tested.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3151316				0	sh	The probability determined by CHIDIST can also be determined by CHITEST.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155123				0	sh	Syntax				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3158439				0	sh	CHIDIST(Number; DegreesFreedom)				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3148675	110			0	sh	            \<emph\>Number\</emph\> is the chi-square value of the random sample used to determine the error probability.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3155615	111			0	sh	            \<emph\>DegreesFreedom\</emph\> are the degrees of freedom of the experiment.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146787				0	sh	Example				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3145774	113			0	sh	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=CHIDIST(13.27; 5)\</item\> equals 0.02.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3156141				0	sh	If the Chi square value of the random sample is 13.27 and if the experiment has 5 degrees of freedom, then the hypothesis is assured with a probability of error of 2%.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id0119200902231887				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>CHISQDIST function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>chi-square distribution\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id0119200901583452				0	sh	CHISQDIST				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id0119200901583471				0	sh	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Returns the value of the probability density function or the cumulative distribution function for the chi-square distribution.\</ahelp\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id0119200902395520				0	sh	Syntax				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id0119200902395679				0	sh	CHISQDIST(Number; Degrees Of Freedom; Cumulative)				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id011920090239564				0	sh	            \<emph\>Number\</emph\> is the number for which the function is to be calculated.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id0119200902395660				0	sh	            \<emph\>Degrees Of Freedom\</emph\> is the degrees of freedom for the chi-square function.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id0119200902395623				0	sh	            \<emph\>Cumulative\</emph\> (optional): 0 or False calculates the probability density function. Other values or True or omitted calculates the cumulative distribution function.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150603				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>EXPONDIST function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>exponential distributions\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150603				0	sh	EXPONDIST				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149563				0	sh	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_EXPONVERT\"\>Returns the exponential distribution.\</ahelp\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153789				0	sh	Syntax				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150987				0	sh	EXPONDIST(Number; Lambda; C)				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3154663	119			0	sh	            \<emph\>Number\</emph\> is the value of the function.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3154569	120			0	sh	            \<emph\>Lambda\</emph\> is the parameter value.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3147332	121			0	sh	            \<emph\>C\</emph\> is a logical value that determines the form of the function. \<emph\>C = 0\</emph\> calculates the density function, and \<emph\>C = 1\</emph\> calculates the distribution.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146133				0	sh	Example				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150357	123			0	sh	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=EXPONDIST(3;0.5;1)\</item\> returns 0.78.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\07090000.xhp	0	help	tit				0	sh	Freeze				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\07090000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150517				0	sh	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/07090000.xhp\" name=\"Freeze\"\>Freeze\</link\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\07090000.xhp	0	help	par_id3156289				0	sh	\<ahelp hid=\".uno:FreezePanes\" visibility=\"visible\"\>Divides the sheet at the top left corner of the active cell and the area to the top left is no longer scrollable.\</ahelp\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060102.xhp	0	help	tit				0	sh	Date & Time Functions				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060102.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154536				0	sh	\<bookmark_value\>date and time functions\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>functions; date & time\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>Function Wizard; date & time\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060102.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154536				0	sh	Date & Time Functions				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060102.xhp	0	help	par_id3153973	2			0	sh	\<variable id=\"datumzeittext\"\>These spreadsheet functions are used for inserting and editing dates and times. \</variable\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060102.xhp	0	help	par_idN10600				0	sh	The functions whose names end with _ADD return the same results as the corresponding Microsoft Excel functions. Use the functions without _ADD to get results based on international standards. For example, the WEEKNUM function calculates the week number of a given date based on international standard ISO 8601, while WEEKNUM_ADD returns the same week number as Microsoft Excel.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060102.xhp	0	help	par_id3150437				0	sh	$[officename] internally handles a date/time value as a numerical value. If you assign the numbering format "Number" to a date or time value, it is converted to a number. For example, 01/01/2000 12:00 PM, converts to 36526.5. The value preceding the decimal point corresponds to the date; the value following the decimal point corresponds to the time. If you do not want to see this type of numerical date or time representation, change the number format (date or time) accordingly. To do this, select the cell containing the date or time value, call its context menu and select \<emph\>Format Cells\</emph\>. The \<emph\>Numbers\</emph\> tab page contains the functions for defining the number format.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060102.xhp	0	help	hd_id2408825				0	sh	Date base for day zero				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060102.xhp	0	help	par_id9988402				0	sh	Dates are calculated as offsets from a starting day zero. You can set the day zero to be one of the following:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060102.xhp	0	help	par_id6401257				0	sh	Date base				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060102.xhp	0	help	par_id5841242				0	sh	Use				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060102.xhp	0	help	par_id6794030				0	sh	'12/30/1899'				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060102.xhp	0	help	par_id7096774				0	sh	(default)				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060102.xhp	0	help	par_id5699942				0	sh	'01/01/1900'				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060102.xhp	0	help	par_id6420484				0	sh	(used in former StarCalc 1.0)				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060102.xhp	0	help	par_id6986602				0	sh	'01/01/1904'				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060102.xhp	0	help	par_id616779				0	sh	(used in Apple software)				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060102.xhp	0	help	par_id791039				0	sh	Choose \<emph\>\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - %PRODUCTNAME Calc - Calculate\</emph\> to select the date base.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060102.xhp	0	help	par_id1953489				0	sh	When you copy and paste cells containing date values between different spreadsheets, both spreadsheet documents must be set to the same date base. If date bases differ, the displayed date values will change!				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060102.xhp	0	help	hd_id757469				0	sh	Two digits years				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060102.xhp	0	help	par_id3149720				0	sh	In \<emph\>\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - $[officename] - General\</emph\> you find the area \<emph\>Year (two digits)\</emph\>. This sets the period for which two-digit information applies. Note that changes made here have an effect on some of the following functions.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060102.xhp	0	help	par_id3150654				0	sh	When entering dates, slashes or dashes used as date separators may be interpreted as arithmetic operators. Therefore, dates entered in this format are not always recognized as dates and result in erroneous calculations. To keep dates from being interpreted as parts of formulas, place them in quotation marks, for example, "07/20/54".				20181231 09:50:43
